# Project goal

Build a server-rendered UK government service using Java, Spring Boot, and Thymeleaf that produces HTML compatible with GOV.UK Frontend.

This project is part of an experiment demonstrating that GOV.UK Frontend does not require Nunjucks, Node.js, or a client-side frontend framework.

The backend rendering technology is an implementation detail.

The compatibility contract is the HTML expected by the official GOV.UK Frontend component fixtures for the exact GOV.UK Frontend version installed by this project.

The objective is to achieve 100% fixture parity for every GOV.UK Frontend component implemented by this project.

## Fundamental architecture

The architecture is:

```text
GOV.UK Frontend fixture
        |
        v
Java component options
        |
        v
Thymeleaf fragment
        |
        v
Server-rendered HTML
        |
        v
DOM comparison
        |
        v
Expected GOV.UK Frontend HTML
```

The application must server-render the complete core experience.

JavaScript may progressively enhance the resulting HTML using the official GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript.

Do not build a client-rendered application.

## Technology stack

Use:

- a current supported LTS Java release
- Spring Boot
- Spring MVC
- Thymeleaf
- Maven
- Maven Wrapper
- JUnit 5
- AssertJ
- Jsoup
- Jackson YAML or SnakeYAML

Use current stable, supported releases unless the repository explicitly pins another version.

Prefer established ecosystem dependencies over small convenience libraries.

Keep dependencies to a sensible minimum.

## GOV.UK Frontend

Install and pin an explicit GOV.UK Frontend version.

GOV.UK Frontend is authoritative for:

- component behaviour
- component options
- expected HTML
- CSS
- JavaScript
- fonts
- images and other assets
- accessibility-related markup
- data attributes
- ARIA attributes

Do not reimplement GOV.UK Frontend CSS.

Do not reimplement GOV.UK Frontend client-side JavaScript.

Do not copy GOV.UK styles into application-specific stylesheets unless an application-specific extension genuinely requires it.

## Component fixtures

Use the official GOV.UK Frontend component fixtures belonging to the exact installed GOV.UK Frontend version.

The fixtures are the compatibility contract.

Before implementing a component:

1. Inspect all upstream fixtures for that component.
2. Inspect its expected HTML.
3. Inspect the upstream Nunjucks macro when necessary to understand rendering behaviour.
4. Design the Java component API from the complete behaviour, not from one example.
5. Implement the component.
6. Run every applicable fixture against it.

The Nunjucks implementation may be studied as a behavioural reference.

Nunjucks must not be required to render the Java implementation.

## Component architecture

Represent GOV.UK component options using strongly typed Java models.

Prefer records for immutable option objects where appropriate.

For example:

```java
public record ButtonOptions(
    String text,
    TrustedHtml html,
    String type,
    String classes,
    Map<String, String> attributes
) {}
```

Use enums where GOV.UK Frontend genuinely defines a constrained set of values.

Do not create enums merely to avoid every string.

Prefer simple, understandable APIs.

Do not expose Thymeleaf implementation details through the public component API.

A component must be renderable independently of a controller or HTTP request.

Keep these concerns separate:

- HTTP routing and controllers
- application/domain logic
- GOV.UK component models
- component rendering
- Thymeleaf templates
- fixture loading
- fixture mapping
- HTML comparison
- tests

Do not put business logic into templates.

