Adapt package names to the application.

Keep GOV.UK rendering code clearly separated from application code.

## Thymeleaf

Use Thymeleaf fragments for GOV.UK components.

Templates must produce the minimum structure required by GOV.UK Frontend.

Do not introduce arbitrary wrapper elements.

Do not change element types merely because they are easier to implement.

Preserve:

- element ordering
- class ordering where required by fixture compatibility
- attributes
- IDs
- names
- values
- ARIA relationships
- data attributes
- error markup
- hint markup
- fieldset and legend relationships
- conditional content
- nested component structures

Avoid complex application logic inside Thymeleaf expressions.

Move complex rendering decisions into the Java component layer when appropriate.

## HTML escaping and security

Plain text must be escaped by default.

A GOV.UK Frontend option named `text` represents text and must not allow arbitrary HTML injection.

Options corresponding to GOV.UK Frontend `html` properties require an explicit trusted HTML representation.

Create a small explicit abstraction such as `TrustedHtml` if required.

Do not represent trusted HTML as an ordinary String when doing so makes accidental unsafe rendering easy.

Never treat arbitrary user-controlled data as trusted HTML.

Do not globally disable Thymeleaf escaping.

Do not use unescaped output simply to make a fixture pass without understanding why the fixture expects HTML.

## Attributes

Support GOV.UK Frontend's arbitrary attributes option where components expose it.

Attributes must be rendered correctly and safely.

Do not create unsafe attribute strings through concatenation.

Pay particular attention to:

- `id`
- `name`
- `value`
- `type`
- `role`
- `aria-*`
- `data-*`
- `disabled`
- `checked`
- `selected`
- `autocomplete`

Boolean HTML attributes must follow valid HTML semantics.

## Fixture test architecture

Fixture testing must be data-driven.

For each fixture:

1. Load the upstream fixture data.
2. Identify the component and fixture.
3. Deserialize its options.
4. Map those options into the Java component model.
5. Render using the Java/Thymeleaf implementation.
6. Obtain the expected GOV.UK Frontend HTML.
7. Parse actual HTML.
8. Parse expected HTML.
9. Compare them.
10. Produce a useful diff when they differ.

A new upstream fixture should normally be testable without creating a new Java test method.

## HTML comparison

Use Jsoup to parse HTML.

Do not rely exclusively on naive raw string equality because template engines can produce semantically irrelevant whitespace differences.

However, normalisation must be conservative.

It may normalise genuinely insignificant differences such as formatting whitespace where HTML semantics are unchanged.

It must not hide differences involving:

- missing elements
- additional elements
- element types
- element ordering
- text
- attributes
- attribute values
- class names
- IDs
- names
- values
- ARIA attributes
- data attributes
- form semantics
- accessibility relationships

Document every normalisation rule.

Every non-trivial normalisation rule must have tests demonstrating both:

1. the irrelevant difference it intentionally ignores
2. meaningful differences it still detects

When a fixture test fails, assume the implementation is wrong before assuming the comparator is wrong.

Do not weaken comparison rules simply to increase the fixture pass rate.

## Testing

Use JUnit 5 and AssertJ.

Tests must cover:

- all supported official fixtures
- default options
- optional options
- text escaping
- trusted HTML
- custom classes
- custom attributes
- IDs
- ARIA
- data attributes
- errors
- hints
- nested content
- form values
- checked states
- selected states
- disabled states
- complex components

Use parameterised tests for fixture suites.

Test the fixture infrastructure itself.

## Forbidden shortcuts

Never:

- modify an upstream fixture to make a test pass
- modify expected HTML to match the implementation
- special-case an individual fixture by fixture name
- hard-code fixture output
- copy pre-rendered fixture HTML into a component
- weaken the comparator to hide a real difference
- invoke Nunjucks at application runtime
- invoke Node.js to generate production component HTML
- call another GOV.UK implementation at runtime
- use React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, or another client-side framework to construct core GOV.UK component markup
- replace GOV.UK Frontend CSS
- replace GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript behaviour unnecessarily

If a fixture cannot be implemented correctly, leave the test failing and document the problem rather than cheating the compatibility test.

## Progressive enhancement

The initial HTTP response must contain the complete core content and controls required for the journey.

Use official GOV.UK Frontend JavaScript to progressively enhance components that require JavaScript behaviour.

The Java implementation is responsible for producing the correct initial HTML.

Do not use JavaScript to repair incorrectly rendered server-side markup.

## Assets

Use the official GOV.UK Frontend package as the source of:

- CSS
- JavaScript
- fonts
- images
- other distributed assets

Provide a reproducible mechanism for obtaining/building those assets.

Pin the GOV.UK Frontend version.

Do not silently load a different version from a CDN.

## Dependency management

Use Maven.

Commit the Maven Wrapper.

Pin important dependencies through normal Maven dependency management.

Avoid unnecessary dependencies.

Run dependency and vulnerability checks where practical.

## Code quality

Prefer:

- immutable data
- records
- clear types
- small focused classes
- constructor injection
- composition
- simple Thymeleaf templates
- explicit behaviour

Avoid:

- static mutable state
- HTML StringBuilder rendering
- reflection-heavy abstractions
- unnecessary inheritance
- excessive generic abstractions
- application logic in templates
- unnecessary JavaScript
- architecture copied directly from TypeScript

Use appropriate Java formatting and static analysis.

Treat compiler and analysis warnings seriously.

## CI

CI must at minimum:

1. compile the application
2. run formatting/static checks
3. run unit tests
4. run all GOV.UK fixture parity tests

A fixture parity failure must fail CI.

## GOV.UK Frontend upgrades

Treat upgrading GOV.UK Frontend as a compatibility event.

When upgrading:

1. update the pinned GOV.UK Frontend version
2. obtain the fixtures belonging to that version
3. run the existing implementation against the new fixtures
4. inspect failures
5. update Java rendering code where GOV.UK behaviour has legitimately changed
6. rerun the complete suite

Do not modify new fixtures to preserve old behaviour.

Fixture failures are useful because they identify compatibility changes.

## Definition of done

A GOV.UK component is complete only when:

- it is rendered by Java and Thymeleaf
- it has no Nunjucks rendering dependency
- it has no Node.js runtime rendering dependency
- it uses official GOV.UK Frontend assets
- every supported upstream fixture passes
- escaping is tested
- trusted HTML behaviour is tested
- accessibility-related markup matches expected output
- fixture comparison has not been weakened
- no fixture-specific hacks exist
- CI passes

The primary success metric is fixture parity, not visual similarity.
